# Python bytecode and caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Runtime state, not source.
# data/ruleforge.db is ~43MB of local history, fixtures and mapping overrides.
# It is created on first run and is machine-specific, so it must never be committed.
data/*.db
data/*.sqlite
data/*.sqlite3

# Editor / agent tooling state
.kilo/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local environment overrides
.env
.env.*

# RuleForge history: pasted detection rules and EVENT SAMPLES, which contain
# hostnames, usernames, source IPs and destination IPs. The server never
# transmits them, but git add -A from a repo with a remote would publish them.
# THESE ARE THE FILES RuleForge WRITES, and this used to name one:
#
#   data/history.json        the history itself
#   data/history.json.lock   a sidecar `history.append` takes a cross-process
#                            lock on, so two copies of RuleForge cannot have one
#                            of them unlink an entry the other was told it had
#                            saved. It holds no history and is 0 bytes on POSIX
#                            and 1 NUL byte on Windows, which is a byte for
#                            `msvcrt.locking` to lock; it is 0600 on POSIX.
#   data/.history-*.tmp      the temp file a rewrite is built in. Exists for
#                            milliseconds, and a process killed mid-rewrite
#                            leaves one behind, which is the one case where a
#                            full copy of the history can sit in `data/`
#                            unmanaged. The prefix is a dot, and the `data/`
#                            rule at the bottom covers all three.
#
# The lock file is deliberately NEVER UNLINKED -- see `history._exclusive` for
# why unlinking a lock is how you end up with two writers each holding one.
#
# THE PERMISSION CLAIM WAS FALSE ON WINDOWS AND IS NOW WRITTEN DOWN. This
# comment used to say "filesystem permissions are 0600" as though that were
# simply true everywhere. It is not:
#
#   POSIX   0600. On the ORDINARY path, which is every append that does not
#           fill the cap -- 1999 of every 2000 -- `history._append_line` passes
#           the mode to `os.open` so the file is never briefly readable, and
#           chmods afterwards as a second statement of the same intent. On the
#           rarer REWRITE path, `history._write` chmods explicitly and
#           `mkstemp` has already created the temp at 0600. The earlier version
#           of this comment named only `_write` and `mkstemp`, which are the
#           path almost nothing takes.
#   win32   `os.chmod` has ONE bit -- the read-only flag -- and does not model
#           POSIX permission bits. The file is exactly as readable as the
#           directory holding it. The real control there is the inherited ACL:
#           `data/` sits under the analyst's own profile, whose default ACL
#           grants that user, SYSTEM and Administrators, and not Everyone.
#
# That is a weaker and differently-shaped guarantee than 0600. It is still the
# reason this rule matters, and `git check-ignore -v data/history.json` is the
# only thing that confirms the rule still matches after any move.
#
# The path is `data/`, NOT `ruleforge/data/`. The package was flattened and an
# ignore rule naming a directory that no longer exists matches NOTHING -- with no
# error, no warning, while `git add -A` starts offering the analyst's pasted
# rules and their machine's hostnames for commit.
data/

